1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,772
Here is a coding agent, Claude Code, talking to a model we host

2
00:00:04,872 --> 00:00:08,767
ourselves, a Qwen model. We looked closely at one pair like this.

3
00:00:09,466 --> 00:00:13,528
Turn one. The agent sends a long fixed block before it ever asks

4
00:00:13,628 --> 00:00:18,016
its real question. Turn two, the same block again. Turn three, again.

5
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:22,473
Across the configurations we measured, that fixed block runs about seventeen

6
00:00:22,573 --> 00:00:25,868
and a half thousand to twenty one thousand tokens, and over

7
00:00:25,968 --> 00:00:29,033
ninety percent of it is tool schemas, not conversation.

8
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:31,824
Some of that cost is already absorbed by prefix

9
00:00:31,924 --> 00:00:35,369
caching. But resending it, and re-processing it, still adds

10
00:00:35,469 --> 00:00:37,652
up. Steno is how we shrink that block.

11
00:00:37,781 --> 00:00:40,939
A correction. Our own load generator capped connections

12
00:00:41,039 --> 00:00:43,369
at one hundred, in both arms. Our earlier

13
00:00:43,469 --> 00:00:46,094
explanation used that number. It is withdrawn.

14
00:00:46,181 --> 00:00:49,743
Cap lifted, sampled counts showed more than one hundred sessions

15
00:00:49,843 --> 00:00:53,348
resident: one hundred twenty three to one hundred thirty seven.

16
00:00:54,247 --> 00:00:57,694
Lifting the cap lowered throughput in three of four cells. At

17
00:00:57,794 --> 00:01:01,647
two tested overload points, one run each, the compressed prompt held

18
00:01:01,747 --> 00:01:05,368
up better: forty seven against eighty point seven at one hundred

19
00:01:05,468 --> 00:01:09,263
twenty eight offered, forty six point three against fifty six point

20
00:01:09,363 --> 00:01:12,112
seven at two hundred fifty six. Cause still open.

